C or shift. Let's evaluate: He might still support C but wants more info on communication. He could argue that while C's communication difficulty is a concern, B's nasty remarks are more severe, making C still preferable. He could also bring up Candidate D's responsibility and problem solving, but also hot temper and know-it-all. He could ask for more info on Candidate D's reliability.\n\nHe should reference explicit facts: C 100% reliable, quick correct decisions, positive crew atmosphere, difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education. B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks. D: responds adequately, concentrates, solves problems, takes responsibility, know-it-all, hot temper.\n\nHe could argue that reliability and decision quality are top, and C leads. He could note that B's memory weakness could affect attention/accuracy. He could note that D's hot temper and know-it-all could affect crew cooperation.\n\nThus he can reaffirm C, but ask for more info on C's communication specifics, maybe ask Sarah for any examples. He could also ask Emily for any observations on D's reliability.\n\nBut the instruction: If need info, use tool calls before public message. He could ask Sarah for more info on C's communication style, but Sarah already said no concrete examples. Could ask Emily for any technical competence evidence for B or D. But maybe not needed.\n\nHe can just make a contribution: \"I remain with C, because reliability and decision speed outweigh communication issues, especially compared to B's interpersonal negativity and D's temper.\" Provide reasoning.\n\nThus produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE: state recommendation for Candidate C, referencing evidence, comparing to B and D, acknowledging concerns but still preferring C.\n\nMETADATA_JSON vote: Candidate C.\n\nProceed."}
